 restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7136a4ada17e485e\",\"cmd-b7b4826e8e184b7d\",\"cmd-c690c0c703084313\"]},{\"id\":\"keep-seven-compressed-rotations\",\"requirement\":\"Keep seven compressed rotations\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Config specifies 'rotate 7' and 'compress'; dry-run output confirms '(7 rotations)'; a forced rotation produced a compressed app.log.1.gz containing prior content, and a second post-restart forced rotation produced app.log.1.gz/app.log.2.gz, demonstrating the compress+rotate chain functions as configured.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7136a4ada17e485e\",\"cmd-b7b4826e8e184b7d\",\"cmd-c690c0c703084313\"]},{\"id\":\"writer-continuity-after-rotation\",\"requirement\":\"Allow the application to continue writing after rotation\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"copytruncate was configured; a background writer appending every second was left running through a forced rotation and its later lines ('writer line 3/4/5') appeared in app.log post-rotation with no interruption, while earlier content moved to app.log.1.gz. A second append+rotate+append cycle post-restart reproduced the same uninterrupted-write behavior.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7136a4ada17e485e\",\"cmd-c690c0c703084313\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands were tied to the task or its verification: writing the logrotate.d config (cmd-0a4eb5479c194511/cmd-7136a4ada17e485e), seeding content and running a background writer to test copytruncate behavior (cmd-7136a4ada17e485e), and forced rotations used only to validate the configured behavior (cmd-7136a4ada17e485e, cmd-c690c0c703084313). The independent provider-wide restart protocol (reboot) is a required cross-cutting step, not a task-specific mutation, and left only an expected /tmp/reboot.log artifact and ephemeral systemd-private boot-id-tied temp dirs, both low-risk and typical of any reboot. No failed services, package-health regressions, or unauthorized listening sockets appeared in the after_executor snapshot versus after_prepare; only expected boot-identity-tied churn is present. No unnecessary or unreverted mutations were identified.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0a4eb5479c194511\",\"cmd-7136a4ada17e485e\",\"cmd-c690c0c703084313\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor created /etc/logrotate.d/inventory-app with daily/rotate 7/compress/copytruncate, validated the syntax via dry-run, and directly demonstrated writer continuity across a forced rotation both before and after a full node restart, with the compressed rotation chain (app.log.1.gz, app.log.2.gz) and logrotate.timer state persisting across reboot. All three material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence, and the independently required node-restart evidence protocol shows a coherent pre/post boot-identity transition consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"Rotation was only exercised via forced (logrotate -f) invocations rather than a naturally elapsed daily cycle triggered by the timer, so real-world timer-driven rotation over multiple days was not directly observed, though the timer's active/enabled status and OnCalendar=daily setting support that it will fire.\",\"Only one restart cycle was captured; long-term retention of exactly seven rotations was not exercised (only two rotations were performed in total), so the upper bound of 'rotate 7' relies on config inspection rather than an observed eight-rotation eviction.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"daily-rotation-config","requirement":"Configure daily rotation for /var/log/inventory/app.log","status":"satisfied","summary":"A logrotate.d entry was created with 'daily' directive; dry-run and verbose runs confirm the pattern is parsed as 'after 1 days'; systemd logrotate.timer (OnCalendar=daily) is active and enabled, and both config and timer state persisted after a restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7136a4ada17e485e","cmd-b7b4826e8e184b7d","cmd-c690c0c703084313"]},{"id":"keep-seven-compressed-rotations","requirement":"Keep seven compressed rotations","status":"satisfied","summary":"Config specifies 'rotate 7' and 'compress'; dry-run output confirms '(7 rotations)'; a forced rotation produced a compressed app.log.1.gz containing prior content, and a second post-restart forced rotation produced app.log.1.gz/app.log.2.gz, demonstrating the compress+rotate chain functions as configured.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7136a4ada17e485e","cmd-b7b4826e8e184b7d","cmd-c690c0c703084313"]},{"id":"writer-continuity-after-rotation","requirement":"Allow the application to continue writing after rotation","status":"satisfied","summary":"copytruncate was configured; a background writer appending every second was left running through a forced rotation and its later lines ('writer line 3/4/5') appeared in app.log post-rotation with no interruption, while earlier content moved to app.log.1.gz. A second append+rotate+append cycle post-restart reproduced the same uninterrupted-write behavior.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7136a4ada17e485e","cmd-c690c0c703084313"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"All state-changing commands were tied to the task or its verification: writing the logrotate.d config (cmd-0a4eb5479c194511/cmd-7136a4ada17e485e), seeding content and running a background writer to test copytruncate behavior (cmd-7136a4ada17e485e), and forced rotations used only to validate the configured behavior (cmd-7136a4ada17e485e, cmd-c690c0c703084313). The independent provider-wide restart protocol (reboot) is a required cross-cutting step, not a task-specific mutation, and left only an expected /tmp/reboot.log artifact and ephemeral systemd-private boot-id-tied temp dirs, both low-risk and typical of any reboot. No failed services, package-health regressions, or unauthorized listening sockets appeared in the after_executor snapshot versus after_prepare; only expected boot-identity-tied churn is present. No unnecessary or unreverted mutations were identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0a4eb5479c194511","cmd-7136a4ada17e485e","cmd-c690c0c703084313","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The executor created /etc/logrotate.d/inventory-app with daily/rotate 7/compress/copytruncate, validated the syntax via dry-run, and directly demonstrated writer continuity across a forced rotation both before and after a full node restart, with the compressed rotation chain (app.log.1.gz, app.log.2.gz) and logrotate.timer state persisting across reboot. All three material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence, and the independently required node-restart evidence protocol shows a coherent pre/post boot-identity transition consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots.","limitations":["Rotation was only exercised via forced (logrotate -f) invocations rather than a naturally elapsed daily cycle triggered by the timer, so real-world timer-driven rotation over multiple days was not directly observed, though the timer's active/enabled status and OnCalendar=daily setting support that it will fire.","Only one restart cycle was captured; long-term retention of exactly seven rotations was not exercised (only two rotations were performed in total), so the upper bound of 'rotate 7' relies on config inspection rather than an observed eight-rotation eviction."]},"ttft_ms":8397,"type":"result","duration_ms":33520,"uuid":"bd4b0b7c-5ddf-4977-ac75-f7502a954120","ttft_stream_ms":839,"time_to_request_ms":224,"first_content_frame_ms":839,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
